Native execution of the cbmc targets (clang, sanitizers, libFuzzer).

	sh native.sh sysv/msg_copy 30
	CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR=/path/to/old/files sh native.sh udf/dirstream 60
	NATIVE_SAN=msan sh native.sh ioctl/ipmi 30

prints one of

	RESULT: CLEAN (N execs, M s)	no finding, every must_reach anchor executed
	RESULT: VACUOUS (N execs, M s)	no finding, an anchor was never executed
	RESULT: FINDING			report above, reproduced in a fresh process
	RESULT: ERROR			build or tool failure

The same target directories serve both engines; nothing in them is
specific to this one, except optional seed inputs in native/seeds/<target>/.

Purpose
	- cross-check cbmc.sh and the common models: a linked binary cannot
	  silently lack a function body, and real sizes are used instead of
	  the harness's scaled-down ones where the code is not bounded by them
	- uninitialised kernel memory reaching copyout (MSan build)
	- deeper loops than CBMC's unwind bounds; fast regression runs

Files
	native.sh	driver, same interface as cbmc.sh
	native/shim.h	CBMC primitives, force-included into every unit
	native/runtime.c	libFuzzer entry point, nondet values, reset, leak check
	native/rewrite.py	LLVM IR rewriting: keep/nobody/replace, stubs, reset
	native/reached.py	coverage of the .i files mapped back to source lines
	native/seeds/	hand-made inputs (ioctl/ipmi, ffs/mountfs)

Build
	1. Preprocess exactly as cbmc.sh does (gcc -E, same flags, config
	   stand-ins, CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR, staged "included" files) plus
	   -include native/shim.h, and -Dmain=harness_main for harness.c.
	   gcc, not clang, preprocesses the kernel units: sys/systm.h maps
	   bcopy to __builtin_bcopy only when !defined(__clang__).  The two
	   models built against the C library (stubs_alloc.c, stubs_user.c)
	   are preprocessed by clang, because glibc headers preprocessed by
	   gcc contain gcc-only attributes.
	2. clang emits LLVM IR with the sanitizer, coverage (fuzzer-no-link)
	   and source-coverage flags, but with -disable-llvm-passes, so no
	   instrumentation has run yet.
	3. rewrite.py edits the IR (below).
	4. clang compiles the rewritten IR, which runs the sanitizer and
	   coverage passes.  ld -r joins kernel, harness, models and stubs
	   into one object; objcopy makes every symbol but harness_main and
	   __native_for_each_global local, so a kernel printf, log or random
	   never binds to libc or to libFuzzer.  Every symbol still undefined
	   must be a C library string/heap function, a sanitizer or fuzzer
	   runtime symbol or a native/runtime.c symbol, otherwise the build
	   stops with "no body and no stub for NAME".
	5. Link with runtime.c and libFuzzer.

Body semantics (rewrite.py), mirroring cbmc.sh
	- A function keeps its body if it is in keep, is defined in the
	  harness translation unit (with "included" targets that is the whole
	  included kernel file: cbmc.sh takes every function of harness.gb),
	  is defined in a header (.h per its debug info), or is one of the
	  fixed model names cbmc.sh keeps.  nobody wins over all of these.
	- Any other defined function gets a body that returns bytes from the
	  input (zero once the input is used up) and does nothing else.  A
	  noreturn function without a body ends the execution like a false
	  __CPROVER_assume.  An sret result is filled the same way.
	- A function declared but defined nowhere gets the same body in
	  stubs.ll; the list is in $W/stubbed.txt.  Exceptions (bound to the
	  C library, checked by the sanitizer, as CBMC uses its own library
	  models for them): mem*, str*, bzero, bcmp, malloc/calloc/realloc/
	  free, abort.
	- A data symbol declared but defined nowhere (extern without a
	  definition) is defined by rewrite.py.  CBMC gives such objects
	  arbitrary content; here they are refilled before every execution
	  with pseudo-random bytes derived from a hash of the input
	  (NATIVE_EXTERN_ZERO=1: zeros).  If the type is incomplete in the
	  declaring unit, 4096 bytes are allocated.
	- replace FUNCTION:MODEL: FUNCTION's body (or generated stub)
	  forwards to MODEL.  As in cbmc.sh, where --replace-calls first
	  turns pointer calls into direct calls, indirect calls reach the
	  model too.
	- fptargets FUNC.function_pointer_call.N/TARGET: before the N-th
	  call through a pointer in FUNC (IR order, which matched CBMC's
	  numbering on every target here) the pointer is compared with the
	  targets; a mismatch is a finding ("fptargets binding"), as CBMC
	  reports it.  The call then goes to whatever the pointer holds.
	- Every writable global and static local of the kernel and harness
	  units is saved at start-up and restored before each execution.

Configuration checks
	As cbmc.sh: the target files must exist, and a function named in
	both keep and nobody is an error.

Process model
	One process runs many inputs (libFuzzer in-process mode); kernel and
	harness globals are restored before each input and every heap object
	the execution allocated is freed after it.  A finding is then rerun
	in a fresh process ("reproduced in a fresh process") so that it
	cannot depend on state left by earlier inputs.  That check caught a
	bug here: the allocation tracking had picked up memory of
	libFuzzer's timer thread and freed it (now only the harness thread
	is tracked).  Measured on
	sysv/msg_copy: about 20000 inputs/s in-process against about 34/s
	when each input starts a fresh process (30 ms per process, mostly
	ASan start-up), so fresh processes per input were not used.

Input layout
	Scalar values (nondet_*(), results of functions without a body) come
	from the front of the input; bulk data (__CPROVER_havoc_slice:
	copyin data, MEMCPY_HAVOC; contents of objects harness.c allocates)
	from the back, in order.  Once the two meet, everything is zero.
	A long copyin therefore does not move later scalars.

CBMC primitives (shim.h, runtime.c)
	__CPROVER_assert, __CPROVER_precondition
		message, file, line, stack trace, abort (a FINDING).  For a
		failed r_ok/w_ok the region is described (ASan).
	__CPROVER_assume(c)
		if false, the execution ends (longjmp), objects it allocated
		are freed, nothing is reported.
	__CPROVER_r_ok/w_ok(p, n)
		false for NULL, wrap-around and n > 2^36; ASan build:
		__asan_region_is_poisoned; then the first and last byte are
		touched, so an unmapped address faults inside the check.
		n == 0 is always true (CBMC: false for an invalid pointer).
	__CPROVER_havoc_slice(p, n)	bulk bytes from the input
	__CPROVER_allocate(n, zero)	calloc/malloc
	__CPROVER_OBJECT_SIZE(p)	__sanitizer_get_allocated_size for a heap
		chunk, 0 for anything else (CBMC: size of any object)
	__builtin_bcopy, __builtin_memcpy, __builtin_memmove are renamed
	(macros in shim.h) to __cbmc_model_*, so the kernel's bcopy/memcpy/
	memmove go through the models in common/stubs_alloc.c as under CBMC.
	clang rejects definitions of __builtin_* names and would expand the
	calls itself otherwise.

Common models
	All of common/stubs_*.c are compiled as they are; there is no native
	replacement of any model.  calloc is routed (shim.h macro) through
	__native_calloc only to learn which objects are zeroed.

Heap contents
	CBMC's malloc returns arbitrary bytes.  Natively, objects allocated
	by harness.c itself (device data, ioctl buffers, results of models)
	take their content from the input (rewrite.py inserts a call after
	each kmalloc/mallocarray/pool_get/__CPROVER_allocate in functions of
	harness.c); zeroed allocations stay zero.  Memory the kernel code
	allocates keeps ASan's fill byte (0xbe) or, in the MSan build, is
	uninitialised.  Filling every allocation from the input was tried
	first: on short inputs kernel buffers ate the bytes meant for later
	nondet values, and usb/ubcmtp never reached its second pipe failure.

Leak check
	When the target's opts contain --memory-leak-check, an execution
	that returns from the harness with heap objects it allocated and did
	not free is a finding ("MEMORY LEAK"), with the allocation stack.
	This is CBMC's notion (allocated and not freed), not LeakSanitizer's
	reachability.  The allocations are tracked with the sanitizer
	malloc/free hooks.

Sanitizers
	asan (default): AddressSanitizer + UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer,
		minus alignment (packed device structures), function (the
		generated stubs carry no type signature) and pointer-overflow
		(the harnesses use arbitrary integers as user addresses, and
		kernel code does arithmetic on them before copyin).  Every
		UBSan report aborts.  UBSan findings are not properties CBMC
		checks; report them separately.
	msan: MemorySanitizer with origin tracking.  The copyout model's
		r_ok check also calls __msan_check_mem_is_initialized on the
		kernel buffer (the shim passes __func__; only copyout asks).
		Limitation: a function without a body does not write its
		out-parameters, which CBMC treats as arbitrary but MSan as
		uninitialised; a report through such a path is an artefact.

Reachability
	The build has source-based coverage.  After a clean run the profile
	is mapped from the .i files back to source lines (reached.py, using
	gcc's line markers) and checked against must_reach as cbmc.sh does;
	an anchor not executed makes the run VACUOUS.  Stubbed functions
	keep their entry counter with step 0, so their lines never count.

Kernel code under clang on Linux
	- implicit declarations, int conversions and incompatible pointer
	  types are errors in clang 18: -Wno-error=... for each.
	- -ffreestanding, otherwise LLVM turns the stubbed kernel printf
	  into putchar/puts, which have no definition.
	- kernel inline assembly: no target here executes any (curcpu() and
	  friends are not on the paths); a target that does would fault.
	- nothing else was needed for the 13 targets.

Limits and deviations from CBMC
	- Undefined data symbols get pseudo-random, not fuzzer-controlled,
	  content.
	- Kernel heap memory is not arbitrary (see Heap contents).
	- Paths that need several exact values in sequence can be out of the
	  fuzzer's reach in a short run: ioctl/ipmi needs a SEND_COMMAND
	  followed by a RECEIVE_MSG, ffs/mountfs a superblock at a fixed
	  offset of an 8 KB block.  Seeds for both are in native/seeds; the
	  layout of a seed depends on the order in which the harness draws
	  values, so a harness change can invalidate it (the result then
	  becomes VACUOUS, not wrong).  The input is consumed in call order
	  by every draw: nondet_* in the harness and the return value of
	  every bodiless function (getuptime, rw_status behind
	  NET_ASSERT_LOCKED, ...), each its type's size, little endian.
	  NATIVE_TRACE=1 lists the stub calls in order; with NATIVE_WORKDIR
	  kept, $W/fuzz FILE runs one candidate (it writes default.profraw
	  into the current directory).  netinet/arp_rtrequest's seed is
	  laid out in the commit that added it.
	- A fuzzer that reaches a harness assertion first may report that
	  and not the sanitizer finding behind it: on the source before a
	  fix, a seed for the path to the memory error shows the latter.
	- Bounds are real: a loop CBMC unwinds N times runs as often as the
	  input says; a non-terminating loop is a libFuzzer timeout (10 s),
	  reported as a FINDING.

Self-test
	The selftest/ targets of cbmc.sh run through native.sh unchanged
	(5 s each, VERIFIED=CLEAN, VIOLATED=FINDING, VACUOUS=VACUOUS).  First
	run: 70 of 72 results agreed; the two others were
	nobody_keep_conflict (no configuration check yet; agrees since) and
	unwind_too_small, which is VIOLATED under CBMC only: an unwinding
	assertion has no native counterpart, the loop simply runs.
	fp_binding_with_replace gave FINDING, but for its result check, which
	the test says must not fail; with the fptargets check it now fails on
	the binding, as under CBMC.  Failure texts differ in general: CBMC's
	"dereference failure" is an ASan report here.

Environment
	NATIVE_SAN, NATIVE_SEED, NATIVE_WORKDIR, NATIVE_TRACE=1 (log stubs and
	path ends), NATIVE_FUZZ_OPTS (extra libFuzzer flags),
	NATIVE_EXTERN_ZERO=1, CBMC_OVERRIDE_DIR.
